+# Round 18 report: correlated roundings and the boundary programme
+
+Status: IN PROGRESS. The round 18 brief and the required round 17 corrections have been read. No proof of Chowla, counterexample, or equivalence is claimed at this checkpoint.
+
+## Scope and exact target
+
+CONJECTURED target: there exists an absolute c > 0 such that every finite nonempty set A of distinct positive integers satisfies M(A) >= c sqrt(n), where n = |A| and M(A) = -min_x sum_{a in A} cos(ax), over the entire real circle.
+
+All round 18 writes are restricted to this report and `tdense18_scratch/`. No root `.research/*.md` files will be edited, no commits or subagents will be used, and all mathematical computation runs on dirichlet through `DIRICHLET_IP=34.148.157.169 src/dirun.sh -c 4`, invoked from bash. No LaTeX.
+
+## Starting frontier
+
+PROVED / QUOTED from P70 and the round 17 report: small M implies energy-weighted P28 spikiness, the quantitative triple lower bound, the graph bulk constraints, and absence of the stated sharp boundary. Full P9 on W2 = {0} union ±A dominates its compressed star and the graph-A bulk bounds. Independent roundings are excluded with high probability when one window carries a fixed proportion of the Bernoulli variance. That theorem does not cover selected correlated outcomes.
+
+PROVED / QUOTED circularity warning: optimizing unrestricted P29(b) gives exactly M already at h = 1, L = 2. Round 18 will measure the explicit sharp-boundary defect instead, and keep sampled P28 distinct from its all-window supremum.
+
+UNKNOWN: whether the four explicit certificates together have a universal square-root lower bound; whether the proposed TAPER statement is true; whether their smallness implies small M in any uniform quantitative sense.
+
+## Work underway
+
+1. Adversarial constructions: sparse cumulative tapers, correlated periodic/Bohr tapers, dilation unions, forced-prefix thinning, algebraic sets, and antithetic selections. Save actual sets and evaluate P28, full W2, compressed star, bulk, and the complete finite optimization over d,k of the sharp-boundary defect.
+2. Derive and adversarially check the structural implication used in TAPER, including losses at vanishing density and the claimed extraction of an arithmetic boundary.
+3. Make the Task 3 implications precise; seek a rigorous separation or a conditional theorem with one explicitly named missing lemma.
+
+NUMERICAL results / live checkpoint: the first VM run is evaluating the saved families. At target sizes 96 and 256, cumulative, antithetic, and Thue–Morse tapers have sampled P28 approximately 0.27–0.29 sqrt(n), small sharp-boundary certificates, but full W2 approximately 0.48–0.69 sqrt(n). Nested Bohr tapers reduce sampled P28 further and increase W2. These finite tests do not establish asymptotics or an all-window P28 upper bound. Any consequential small-M observation must receive an all-circle directed-arithmetic certificate before use as a construction claim.
+
+## Exact finite reductions being verified
+
+PROVED (elementary; new packaging in this report). The complete positive sharp-boundary optimization is finite without a cutoff assumption on X. Put c0 = 2/(3 pi) - 1/8. Since D_d(k) >= k - |A_d|,
+
+    c0 k - D_d(k) <= |A_d| - (1-c0)k.
+
+Thus a positive value requires d to divide some a in A and k < |A_d|/(1-c0). The VM code enumerates all these d,k and computes D as an exact rational, using rational lower and upper bounds for c0. This is a complete boundary audit, unlike a scan of a few dilations or scales.
+
+PROVED (elementary). For the Fejer triangular version of P28, all integer H and centers reduce to 1 <= H <= X and H <= m < X+H. For H >= X write m=H+r. Then
+
+    V_(H,H+r)(x) = (1/H) sum_{a>r} (a-r) exp(iax).
+
+Its centerwise supremum decreases as 1/H, so H=X dominates every H>=X. This permits an all-window, rather than sampled-window, upper certificate on a finite example. The reduction concerns triangular Fejer kernels; it does not optimize every other kernel allowed by general P28.
+
+## Structural review after the first two attacks
+
+PROVED / logic: a one-sided certificate implication does not make the programme equivalent to Chowla. The current known implication is small M => all four small certificates. The reverse implication requires proof. Conversely, even a uniform cover of the four certificates could prove Chowla without providing an upper comparison M <= C times their maximum. These are different assertions.
+
+Decision: continue the sparse deterministic constructions, but also test the reverse implication directly on the round 17 optimized finite set using all triangular windows, exact graph positivity, the exhaustive boundary audit, and certified M. Separately derive a theorem for correlated selectors with independent blocks; this addresses antithetic selection without silently reusing independence of individual frequencies.
+
+## Correlated selectors with bounded block occupancy
+
+PROVED on paper (round 18; constants undergoing independent exact moment checks). Let the potential positive frequencies be partitioned into disjoint finite blocks. The selector vectors on different blocks are independent; dependence within each block is arbitrary. In block j at most b_j frequencies are selected, put b = max_j b_j >= 1 and n0 = sum_j b_j, and let p_a be the marginal inclusion probabilities. For any one permissible triangular P28 window w_a, put
+
+    v = sum_a p_a(1-p_a) w_a^2,
+    B = (1/2) average_x |sum_a w_a X_a exp(iax)|.
+
+Then, for v>0,
+
+    M(A) >= B,
+    expectation B >= v / (2 sqrt(6 b n0 + 4 b^2)),
+    variance B <= b/2,
+    probability[B < v/(4 sqrt(6 b n0 + 4 b^2))]
+        <= 8 b (6 b n0 + 4 b^2) / v^2.
+
+Here M(empty)=0. If b is bounded and v >= c n0 for a fixed c>0, this gives a square-root bound with probability tending to one, uniformly in the sizes of the frequency blocks and in the density. It covers independent antithetic pairs and one-point-per-cell sampling. It does not cover a globally selected deterministic outcome or a single block containing all n selected points.
+
+Proof. Symmetrize with an independent selector copy. For each block let Y_j(x) be its weighted Fourier difference and Q(x)=sum_j expectation|Y_j(x)|^2. Independence and symmetry give
+
+    expectation|sum_j Y_j|^4 <= 3 Q(x)^2 + sum_j expectation|Y_j|^4.
+
+The occupancy bounds imply Q(x)<=2 sum_j b_j^2<=2 b n0 and |Y_j(x)|<=2b. Parseval, which removes all cross-frequency covariance terms even within a block, gives average_x Q(x)=2v. Consequently the joint fourth moment is at most (6 b n0+4 b^2)2v. Holder interpolation gives the mean bound after the two symmetrization factors of 1/2. For the variance, the functional B on real coefficient vectors is convex and 1/2-Lipschitz in Euclidean norm. If only block j is resampled, the squared displacement is at most 2b_j. Convexity and block Efron–Stein therefore bound variance B by 2b times the squared subgradient norm, hence by b/2. Chebyshev gives the displayed probability. These arguments use independence of blocks only.
+
+PROVED accompanying identity, for arbitrary correlated selectors: the circle average of the expected squared centered window sum is exactly v. Antithetic cancellation cannot remove this Parseval energy; it can move the energy into a thinner set of angles. Thus “hidden variance” must mean spectral concentration, rather than smaller coefficient variance.
+
+## A deterministic theorem: periodic triangular Bohr tapers have a linear obstruction
+
+PROVED on paper (round 18; exact finite witness checks underway). Let L be a positive multiple of 20, P>=100 an integer, q>=4L+1 an integer, and r_q(a) the centered residue modulo q. Define the genuine Boolean set
+
+    A(L,q,P) = {1<=a<=Pq : |r_q(a)| <= L(1-a/(Pq))},
+    n = |A(L,q,P)|.
+
+Then the full W2 certificate, and hence M(A), satisfy
+
+    -lambda_min(Cay[{0} union ±A])/2 >= 7n/139400 + 1/2.
+
+The constant is independent of the density, the modulus, and the number of periods. In particular choose q=L^2 and P=L^2 and let L tend to infinity through multiples of 20. Then n is of order L^3 and density is of order 1/L, the critical n^(-1/3) regime. This explicitly excludes that correlated nested-Bohr family; it does not exclude all moving Bohr windows.
+
+Proof. Write h=L/20 and J=floor(P/100). Take three packets inside A:
+
+    U = {jq+r : 1<=j<=J, -16h<=r<-14h},
+    V = {jq+r : 1<=j<=J, -h<=r<h},
+    W = {jq+r : 1<=j<=J, 14h<=r<16h}.
+
+Each has m=2hJ=(L/10)J elements. Their frequencies are at most Jq+16h, hence at most 0.012Pq, and their centered residues have magnitude at most 0.8L. Thus all three packets lie in A. For any two packet elements, the magnitude of their difference is at most Jq+32h <= 0.014Pq, so the allowable residue radius for that difference is at least 0.986L. Residue differences between U and V, or V and W, have magnitude less than 0.85L. Differences inside a packet have magnitude less than 0.1L. All these distinct pairs are therefore edges. Residue differences between U and W exceed 1.4L and are less than q/2, so these pairs are never edges. There is no modular wrap in any of these tests because q>=4L+1.
+
+Assign vector values 2 on U, -3 on V, and 2 on W. Its squared norm is 17m, and the exact adjacency quadratic form is -7m^2-17m. P9 gives M >= 7m/34+1/2. Finally J>=P/200 and n<=(2L+1)P<=(41/20)LP imply m>=n/4100, giving the stated bound. For the displayed sparse sequence, the same cardinality upper bound and a fixed fraction of the first P/2 periods with |r|<=L/4 give n of order LP=L^3. No limiting theorem is needed for the spectral bound.
+
+PROVED / mechanism: smooth tapering along the long coordinate does not smooth the transverse Boolean cutoff. The cutoff creates three large packets with the adjacency pattern just exhibited, which full W2 detects. An extraction of a sharp *one-dimensional* boundary in some A_d is unnecessary for this family.
+
+Novelty scope: P9 and the negative eigenvalue of an induced three-vertex path were already in P70.7. The independent-block estimate and this explicit density-uniform packet construction were not found in the required grep of PROOFS. No outside priority claim, kernel check, or independent human review is asserted. This theorem is stronger than a numerical exclusion but is not a universal square-root theorem.
